### Test Redis Caching

# 1. Get all games (first request - cache miss, will query database)
GET http://localhost:5000/games
Accept: application/json

###

# 2. Get all games again (second request - cache hit, faster response)
GET http://localhost:5000/games
Accept: application/json

###

# 3. Get specific game by ID (first request - cache miss)
GET http://localhost:5000/games/1
Accept: application/json

###

# 4. Get specific game by ID (second request - cache hit)
GET http://localhost:5000/games/1
Accept: application/json

###

# 5. Create a new game (will invalidate "games:all" cache)
POST http://localhost:5000/games
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Cyberpunk 2077",
  "genre": "RPG",
  "description": "Open world RPG set in Night City",
  "releaseDate": "2020-12-10"
}

###

# 6. Get all games again (cache was invalidated, will query database)
GET http://localhost:5000/games
Accept: application/json

###

# 7. Update a game (will invalidate both "games:all" and "games:id:1" caches)
PUT http://localhost:5000/games/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Elden Ring - Updated",
  "genre": "RPG",
  "description": "Epic fantasy action RPG - Game of the Year Edition",
  "releaseDate": "2022-02-25"
}

###

# 8. Delete a game (will invalidate caches)
DELETE http://localhost:5000/games/3

###

# Pro Tip: Check response times in VS Code
# - First request (cache miss): ~20-50ms
# - Subsequent requests (cache hit): ~1-5ms
# - Look for the time shown in VS Code's HTTP file response
